<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taxila</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taxila was an ancient city in the north-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, known as a centre of trade and learning. It lay on routes connecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> name it, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, as one of the urban centres that contributed to craft production, coinage and long-distance commerce.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taxila.jpg" alt="Taxila" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sasha Isachenko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dharmarajika_stupa,Taxila.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Taxila, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> as Takṣaśilā, lies on the Pothohar Plateau in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, about 25 kilometres north-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamabad">Islamabad</a> and Rawalpindi area, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. The Sanskrit name is translated as City of Cut Stone or Rock of Taksha. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> the city was founded by Bharata, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, and named for his son Taksha. The modern name derives from the Greek rendering recorded in Ptolemy's Geography.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Settlement in the Taxila valley goes back to the late 4th or early 3rd millennium BCE at Sarai Khola, and the first major settlement, on the Hathial mound, was established around 1000 BCE. Taxila was a capital of the Gandhara kingdom, together with Puṣkalāvatī, though at times it formed its own district or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>. It may have grown considerably by the time of the Achaemenid conquest of the 6th century BCE. In 326 BCE its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambhi">Taxiles</a>, called Āmbhi, surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> without a battle; Greek historians described the city as wealthy, prosperous and well governed, and Alexander kept Taxiles as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a>.</p><p>Within about a decade of Alexander's departure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> captured the city. Buddhist texts say <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Chanakya</a> came from Taxila, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, the grandson of Chandragupta, it became a great seat of Buddhist learning. It lay on the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-trunk-road">Grand Trunk Road</a> linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. In the 2nd century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greeks</a> built a new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirkap">Sirkap</a>, across the river, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maues">Maues</a> overthrew the last Greek king of Taxila around 90 BCE. Inscriptions of 76 CE show Kushan rule, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a> later founded Sirsukh, the latest of the ancient settlements, when the city was known for trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, sandalwood, horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, pearls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huna-people">Hunas</a> devastated Taxila in the 5th century, and by the time the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> described it in the 7th century it was in decline. Its ruins were rediscovered in the mid-19th century by Alexander Cunningham and extensively excavated by Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-marshall">John Marshall</a>. UNESCO designated Taxila a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1980. Some scholars regard the so-called university of ancient Taxila as one of the earliest centres of education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taxila” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his death the Bactrian kings Pantaleon and Agathocles struck bilingual coins found as far as <b>Taxila</b>, and civil wars among them allowed Apollodotus I, from about 180/175 BCE, to rule Gandhara and western Punjab as the first king independent of Bactria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Urban life flourished at <b>Taxila</b>, Peshawar, Mathura, Termez and Bactra, and irrigation works expanded farming in Bactria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Urban centres such as Pataliputra, <b>Taxila</b> and Ujjain lived on craft production, silver punch-marked coinage and long-distance commerce.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The story is told as a frame tale: Vaisampayana, a disciple of Vyasa, first recites it at <b>Taxila</b> to King Janamejaya, great-grandson of Arjuna, and many years later the storyteller Ugrashrava Sauti recites it again to sages at a twelve-year sacrifice in the Naimisha Forest.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>Gondophares, a ruler of Iranian, probably Parthian, origin from Sistan, built a kingdom in the early 1st century by taking Arachosia and then Gandhara and <b>Taxila</b> from the Indo-Scythians.</blockquote><a href="/state/indo-parthian-kingdom">Indo-Parthian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 85 BCE their king Maues established himself in Gandhara and took <b>Taxila</b> from the Indo-Greek Kingdom, founding a Saka kingdom in north-western India.</blockquote><a href="/state/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a></li></ul></section>
